```text
RECORD OF PROCEEDINGS FOR THE GOVERNING BODY
OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE

Office of City Clerk

March 2, 2017

The Governing Body of the City of Cheyenne convened in a special meeting on this date
beginning at 6:10 p.m. in City Council Chambers for the purpose of collective labor bargaining
with the Cheyenne Professional Fire Fighters, International Association of Fire Fighters, Local
No. 279.  Present were:  MAYOR - Marian J. Orr; COUNCIL MEMBERS - Rocky Case
(leaving the meeting at 6:42 p.m.), Richard Johnson, Mike Luna, Dr. Mark Rinne (arriving at
6:37 p.m.), Scott Roybal (arriving at 7:58 p.m.), Dicky Shanor and Jeff White. Absent: Bryan
Cook and Pete Laybourn. Also present were:  Kylie Soden, Deputy City Clerk; Sylvia Hackl,
City Attorney; Lois Huff, City Treasurer, and Tom Schingle, Interim Chief, Fire and Rescue
Department.  The pledge of allegiance was recited.

Mayor Orr explained the minimum number of members was present for a quorum of the
governing body, and there would be a member leaving and one joining the special meeting so a
quorum would be maintained. She advised the meeting would be audio recorded, and stated that
Wyoming State Statute §16-4-405(a)(x) of the Public Meetings Act allows the governing body to
meet in executive session to consider or tender offers regarding salaries, wages or other terms
and conditions of employment during negotiations with the fire fighters union and that any
motion approved by the governing body to move into executive session during the special
meetings would be pursuant to this section of statute.  Mayor Orr further advised if the governing
body met in executive session, they would be meeting in the City Council Administrative office,
and that Fire Union representatives were welcome to use Room 104 for the purpose of meeting
to caucus.  She advised that spokespersons for the City are herself and Dicky Shanor, and
Jonathan Bertsch and Andrew Dykeshorn are the spokespersons for Local Union #279.  Any
collective labor agreement that may be reached must be approved by the governing body with
the agreement placed on the city council agenda for approval.

Mayor Orr presented information on the City’s negotiation team (on file with the City
Clerk’s office) to the Union and asked if they would accept the City’s initial proposal of a 1 year
extension of the current contract including a 1% increase to base pay. Mr. Bertsch advised that
they reject the offer of the City’s initial proposal, and asked if the City accepts the Union’s initial
proposal.  Mr. Shanor advised the City would like to hear the Union’s priorities before meeting
in executive session to confer on the proposed offer.  Mr. Shanor suggested working from the
Union’s proposal moving forward, and Mr. Bertsch agreed.  Mr. Bertsch explained there are
amendments within their proposal that are affiliated with a cost, and some that aren’t, and that
the Union believes that the current contract is in need of some revisions. The Union’s top general
priorities are: life safety (citizens and members of the department), employee retention and
listening to the City’s reference points.  High priority sections within the contract that are cost
affiliated are: Section 22, Classification of Employment and Basic Wages; Section 15, Company
Strength, and Section 27, Wellness/Fitness Program. Important sections within the contract that
have low or no cost are all of the remaining proposed sections with changes.  Regarding the
```

### Page 2 of 3

```text
City’s previous request to present information on cost vs. revenue, Mr. Bertsch explained that
costs will ultimately be calculated through the City’s projections, and they are trying to avoid
costs affiliated with losing employees. He provided information on proposed Section 46,
Wildland, explaining that by the end of 2019, the program can potentially be operating “in the
black” with startup fees being covered and a net gain being realized by the department and City.
Mr. Shanor moved to meet in executive session, seconded by Mr. Johnson. Motion carried.
Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present. [City Clerk note: Audio recording of
the meeting was stopped; executive session convened at 6:33 p.m. in the City Council
Administrative office]

Following closure of the executive session, Mayor Orr reconvened the special meeting at
8:08 p.m. [City Clerk note: Dr. Rinne joined executive session at 6:37 p.m.; Mr. Case left
executive session at 6:42 p.m., and Mr. Roybal joined executive session at 7:58 p.m.]. She stated
the City’s priories are overtime predictability, management rights and sustainability of the
service structure, and provided an explanation for each priority.  Mr. Shanor advised that the City
rejects the Union’s proposed offer, and will work from the Union’s proposal presenting
counteroffers (copies provided and on file with the City Clerk’s Office), which following
conclusion of the special meeting, can be incorporated into one working document.  The
following sections were presented and explained to the Union with counteroffers: Section 2,
Definitions; Section 13; Management Rights; Section 15, Company Strength; Section 17,
Severance Pay; Section 20, Inclement Weather; Section 22, Classification of Employment and
Basic Wages; Section 24, Working in a Higher Job Rank; Section 27, Wellness/Fitness Program;
Section 28, Vacations; Section 33, Uniforms; Section 46, Wildland, and Section 47,
Labor/Management Relations.  The City had no changes to existing language in all other
sections.  The Union asked for clarification on Section 47, Labor Management Committee,
relative to how many representatives the City will have, and Section 28, Vacations, subsection J,
the City’s proposed language: “and in a manner that will minimize overtime as determined by
the Fire Chief.”  Mayor Orr advised this is a function of management, including scheduling and
minimizing overtime as much as possible. Mayor Orr recessed the special meeting at 8:38 p.m.
to allow Union representatives to caucus.

Mayor Orr reconvened the special meeting at 10:05 p.m.  In response to the governing
body’s previously noted proposals, the Union presented counteroffers to the following sections:
Section 2, Definitions; Section 13, Management Rights; Section 15, Company Strength; Section
17, Severance Pay; Section 22, Classification of Employment and Basic Wages; Section 24,
Working in a Higher Job Rank; Section 27, Wellness/Fitness Program; Section 28, Vacations;
Section 33, Uniforms; Section 46, Wildland, and Section 47, Labor/Management Relations.

It was advised that the next special meeting would be held March 6, 2017, and that an
updated “shared” proposal would be provided.

Mayor Orr stated the purpose of the special meeting had been met, and there being no
other business previously noted for the special meeting, adjourned the meeting at 10:50 p.m.
```

